{# Template: capabilities-reasoner/capability-register.j2 #}
{# Functional Role: KnowAct — Build/load typed capability registry #}
{# Produces: registry #}
{# Phase 1 — REGISTER (PLAN: build capability ontology) #}
{# hKask v0.36.0 — Capabilities Reasoner #}
{#
  The registry is your model of what capabilities SHOULD exist and their
  limits. Without it, you can only measure performance, not reason about
  capabilities. A weak registry produces unprincipled verdicts; a strong
  registry produces actionable ones.

  Three capability domains, each with its own exemplar tradition:
    - PSYCHOLOGICAL (Dunning [kruger-dunning-1999]): the metacognitive
      gap — skills to perform = skills to evaluate. Self-assessment is
      unreliable for capabilities the system lacks.
    - ECONOMIC (Sen [sen-1999], Nussbaum [nussbaum-2000]): capability =
      feasible functionings; conversion factors mediate resource →
      functioning; Nussbaum's 10 capabilities have floor thresholds.
    - COMPUTE (Miller [miller-2006], AIP [aip-2026]): object-capability —
      only connectivity begets connectivity; authority only attenuates.

  Three limit types per capability:
    - FLOOR (Nussbaum [nussbaum-2000]): below threshold → deprivation →
      must expand. Metacognitive dimension (Dunning [kruger-dunning-1999]):
      a system below floor on a metacognitive capability cannot diagnose
      its own deprivation — detect externally.
    - CEILING (AIP [aip-2026] / Akselrod [akselrod-2023]): above threshold
      → restriction warrant. Attenuation rule (Miller [miller-2006]):
      authority only narrows.
    - MATURITY (CMMI [cmmi]): prerequisite DAG — level N requires level N−1.

  Feedback loop: when prior_iteration is present, consume its
  next_registry_focus to refine this registry rather than starting
  from scratch.
#}

[inference]
temperature = 0.3
work_effort = "high"
verbosity = "standard"
